--TEST--
A write through a `property<T>` is `E0782` where `T`'s public set holds a `readonly` property, even when the key holds another name, while a read through the same key compiles — `rule:types/property-key-access`
--FILE--
<?nvs
class Order {
    public readonly int $id;
    public string $status = "new";

    public function constructor(int $id) {
        $this->id = $id;
    }
}

Order $order = new Order(7);

// The read compiles, and the key names the assignable property.
property<Order> $key = "status" as property<Order>;
echo $order->$key, "\n";

// The write is refused at the write, naming the `readonly` property, because
// the key's type cannot say which of the set's names it holds.
$order->$key = "paid";
// covers: lang:types/property-keys
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0782]: `Order::$id` is `readonly`, so only `Order`'s constructor writes it
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
